australia's bushland is still recovering  from the black summer fires that ripped  through the land a year ago  blackening 35 million hectares and  killing or displacing  nearly 3 billion animals soon after  logging was restarted in fire affected  state forests  many plant species and almost all animal  species simply can't deal with that  double disturbance in rapid succession  and that makes salvage logging or post  fire logging  the most detrimental form of logging  of all kinds of harvesting harvesting  standards were tightened after the fires  but in the south brumen state forest  locals found evidence of loggers  breaching their licence  allegedly cutting down large  hollow-bearing trees  which provide crucial shelter for  threatened species  authorities are investigating but  activists have continued their campaign  to end logging in the region we were  shocked we were just shocked that that  level of non-compliance was going on and  that  forestry corporation itself hadn't  picked it up the new south wales  forestry corporation says its operations  are sustainable and  carefully managed with forests  regenerating after being logged  but as the climate heats up so too has  debate  on the future of logging and how  australia is more broadly managing its  land  the world wildlife fund has listed  australia as a global hot spot for  deforestation  with land being cleared mainly for  agriculture  and farming whether it's land clearing  for farming or logging in state forests  like this one many environmentalists  believe  if australia is serious about action on  climate change  it needs to rethink how it manages its  bush land and forests  or they warn habitats and wildlife are  at risk  of irreparable damage recent research  has also found evidence that logging  native forests is making fires more  severe from debris  left behind and forests are important  sources of carbon storage  vital to reducing emissions it's the  greatest  source of carbon sink the the mature  trees  so without them what do we do  you know what are we going to do with  australian leaders under pressure to  address climate change the question will  be how the nation manages its land  while also balancing business interests  nicola gage aljazeera south bruman state  forest
 

